  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe will optimize a piecewise constant sequence using a constrained optimization technique that leverages the properties of auto-correlation in convolutional transforms. The central idea is to decompose the sequence into characteristic intervals and balance local maximums while maintaining global auto-correlation constraints. This approach combines interval-based parameterization with derivative-free optimization to handle the high-dimensional, non-convex landscape of the auto-correlation maximization problem. The method addresses the bottleneck of local maxima in traditional gradient-based methods by introducing interval-wise parameterization and adaptive search spaces.\n\nJustification:\n- Piecewise constant steps provide a compact representation while affording mathematical tractability\n- Frequency-space analysis via convolution helps identify structure-damaging components\n- Dual-constrained optimization balances energy concentration against auto-correlation growth\n- Interval-wise optimization prevents falling into trivial local optima\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: Nonnegative sequence f(x) = \u03a3_i h_i 1_{[a_i, b_i]} where h_i, a_i, b_i are parameters\n- Normed space: ||f||_2 = \u221a(\u222bf(x)^2 dx) = \u221a2N for normalization\n- Auto-correlation: R(\u03c4) = convolve(f,f)(\u03c4) = \u03a3_t f(x)f(t+x+\u03c4)\n- Optimization problem:\n  minimize max_\u03c4 R(\u03c4) subject to\n    \u222bf(x)^2 dx = \u221a2N\n    f(x) \u2265 0\n    individual h_i \u2264 Cap\n\nImplementation details:\n- Uses Parseval's identity to compute energy and auto-correlation\n- Formulates dual problem in frequency space\n- Implements projected gradient with interval constraints\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nPhase 1: Initialization\n- Start with uniform grid of step functions\n- Clip values: max(0, min(Cap, initial_guess))\n- Formulate dual constraints using FFT\n  - Compute frequency transform F(\u03c9) = \u221a(2\u03c0) F_T(\u03c9)\n  - Project to feasible domain via soft-thresholding\n\nPhase 2: Iterative Refinement\n- While time remaining:\n  - Solve projected gradient step\n    x_{k+1} = Proj_{\u2264Cap}(x_k - \u03b1\u2207L(x_k))\n  - Update via dual variable boost:\n    \u03bb_{k+1} = \u03bb_k(1 + \u03b3||\u2207L||^2)\n  - Early termination if:\n    max(|\u0394x|, |\u0394L|) < tol or time limit\n\nPhase 3: Candidate Validation\n- When no improvement:\n  - Sample via random simplex perturbation\n  - Compute exact_score() across all 2N-1 points\n  - Verify with eigenspace projection\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Adaptive grid refinement:\n  - Candidate survival if improvision > 0.5%\n  - Coefficients evolve with Gaussian smoothing \u03c3 = 0.1\u221a(\u0394L)\n- Dual-parameter strategy:\n  - Primary: High-pass filter boost \u03b2=1.8\n  - Secondary: Auto-correlation clipping factor \u03b3=0.95\n- Hybrid method alternation:\n  - 70% projected gradient\n  - 20% interval swapping\n  - 10% random restarts\n\n5. Budget Allocation and Timing\n- Timeline apportionment:\n  - 300s: Grid initialization\n  - 500s: Main optimization\n  - 300s: Validation & polishing\n  - 200s: Fallback mechanisms\n- Memory profiling:\n  - Track active dimension reduction\n  - Monitor solver workspace size\n  - Enable dynamic FFT size adaptation\n\n6. Validation and Return\n- Precompute check_points at:\n    N = original length-2, length, length+2\n- Fallback procedure:\n  - Normalize current_best to ||x||_2 = \u221a2N\n  - Clip at Cap=1000\n  - Return collection of 3 points\n- Success criteria:\n  - Valid score < 1.55 accepted\n  - Time safety margin: 100s reserved for validation\n  - Curvature check: Hessian at optimum estimated via BJORKSTEN\n\nThe algorithm maintains a dual-check reservoir to capture potentially superior solutions across frequency components. Convergence is determined by both metric reduction and phase space volume reduction.",
